story += [Spacer(1,2*cm),Paragraph('Content-Based Fingerprint Retrieval System for Criminal Identification Using Deep Feature Extraction',styles['CoverX']),Spacer(1,0.8*cm),Paragraph('A Project Report Submitted',styles['CenterX']),Paragraph('in Partial Fulfilment of the Requirements for the Degree of',styles['CenterX']),Paragraph('[DEGREE AND PROGRAMME]',styles['CenterX']),Spacer(1,0.8*cm),Paragraph('Submitted by',styles['CenterX']),Paragraph('[STUDENT NAME(S)]<br/>[REGISTRATION NUMBER(S)]',styles['CoverX']),Paragraph('Under the guidance of',styles['CenterX']),Paragraph('[GUIDE NAME AND DESIGNATION]',styles['CenterX']),Spacer(1,1*cm),Paragraph('[DEPARTMENT]<br/>[INSTITUTION]<br/>[CITY, COUNTRY]',styles['CoverX']),Paragraph('[MONTH YEAR]',styles['CenterX'])]
def front(title,text):
    story.extend([PageBreak(),Paragraph(title,styles['ChapterX']),P(text)])
front('UNDERTAKING','I/We, [STUDENT NAME(S)], declare that the work presented in this report, entitled <i>Content-Based Fingerprint Retrieval System for Criminal Identification Using Deep Feature Extraction</i>, is our work carried out under [GUIDE NAME]. Sources and contributions of others have been acknowledged. This declaration and the final submission status must be reviewed and signed by the student(s).<br/><br/>Date: [DATE]<br/>Place: [PLACE]<br/><br/>Signature(s): [STUDENT SIGNATURE(S)]')
front('CERTIFICATE','This is to certify that the project report entitled <i>Content-Based Fingerprint Retrieval System for Criminal Identification Using Deep Feature Extraction</i> has been submitted by [STUDENT NAME(S), REGISTRATION NUMBER(S)] under the guidance of [GUIDE NAME] to [DEPARTMENT, INSTITUTION]. The certificate is subject to verification and signature by the authorised faculty.<br/><br/>[GUIDE SIGNATURE] &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; [HEAD OF DEPARTMENT SIGNATURE]<br/>Date: [DATE]')
front('Acknowledgment','I/We thank [GUIDE NAME], [DEPARTMENT], and [INSTITUTION] for their guidance and support. We acknowledge the authors of the SOCOFing dataset, the open-source software used in this work, and the researchers cited in this report. [Replace or extend this acknowledgement with the contributors and support actually received.]')
front('Abstract','This project investigates content-based retrieval of fingerprints using fixed-length deep embeddings. A DenseNet121 backbone, selected from twelve trained checkpoints using a sampled equal-error-rate (EER) comparison, maps preprocessed 128 by 128 pixel fingerprint images to 512-dimensional, L2-normalised vectors. An ArcFace objective is used during training. Embeddings and image-level metadata are stored in a cosine-similarity Pinecone index; nearest-neighbour search returns ranked candidate fingers. The processed SOCOFing-derived splits contain 38,808 training, 8,283 validation, and 8,179 test images. On 1,000 genuine and 1,000 impostor test pairs, the selected checkpoint achieved a sample EER of 0.0%. In a separate 100-person, 1,000-query retrieval experiment spanning validation and test subjects, the original second, fourth, and sixth positions matched the finger class in 99.9%, 99.7%, and 99.4% of queries, respectively, after removing the self-match. These outcomes are specific to the recorded samples and synthetic alterations; they do not establish forensic accuracy on independent latent prints. The system provides candidate retrieval for further assessment, not proof of identity.')
story += [PageBreak(),Paragraph('Contents',styles['ChapterX']),listing('TOCEntry'),PageBreak(),Paragraph('List of Figures',styles['ChapterX']),listing('FigureEntry'),PageBreak(),Paragraph('List of Tables',styles['ChapterX']),listing('TableEntry'),PageBreak(),Paragraph('List of Abbreviations',styles['ChapterX'])]
story += table('Table A: Abbreviations',[['Abbreviation','Meaning'],['AFIS','Automated Fingerprint Identification System'],['API','Application Programming Interface'],['ArcFace','Additive angular-margin training loss'],['CBIR','Content-based image retrieval'],['CLAHE','Contrast limited adaptive histogram equalisation'],['CNN','Convolutional neural network'],['DFD','Data-flow diagram'],['EER','Equal error rate'],['FAR / FRR','False acceptance / false rejection rate'],['mAP','Mean average precision'],['ROC / AUC','Receiver operating characteristic / area under curve'],['ROI','Region of interest']],[130,300])
story += chapter(1,'Introduction')
story += [P('Fingerprint impressions contain friction-ridge arrangements that can support comparison of an unknown print with known exemplars. In forensic practice, the examiner assesses the clarity and correspondence of ridge detail, documents the comparison and follows appropriate verification procedures. Automated searches can narrow a candidate list, but a similarity score alone cannot establish source identity [1], [2]. This project examines a research prototype that represents a fingerprint by a learned numerical vector and retrieves nearby vectors.'),H('1.1 Background and motivation'),P('Fingerprint collections can be searched with minutiae-based representations, ridge-image representations, or learned features. Minutiae such as ridge endings and bifurcations are informative but can be difficult to detect reliably in noisy, partial or distorted impressions. Fixed-length representations offer a convenient interface to vector search and can be computed without an explicit minutiae graph [3]. Their usefulness depends on training, data quality, and the similarity between evaluation data and the intended deployment setting.'),P('Content-based image retrieval (CBIR) compares measurable image content rather than textual file names. In this system the content descriptor is a CNN embedding: the same processing and model must be applied to both the query and enrolled images. The index returns vectors with high cosine similarity and the associated metadata identifies candidate fingers.'),H('1.2 Problem statement'),P('Given a fingerprint image and a gallery of enrolled fingerprint images, retrieve a ranked set of candidate fingers whose learned image representations are similar to the query. The research challenge is to preserve finger-specific evidence under image alteration and suppress similarity between distinct fingers. The operational challenge is to retain vectors persistently and map each search result back to the correct source image and finger class.'),H('1.3 Aim and objectives'),P('The aim is to develop and evaluate a content-based fingerprint candidate-retrieval pipeline. The specific objectives are to preprocess fingerprint images consistently; train and compare CNN feature extractors; generate and normalise 512-dimensional embeddings; store image-level vectors and metadata in Pinecone; retrieve similarity-ranked candidates; and measure verification and identification behaviour using documented experimental protocols.'),H('1.4 Scope and contributions'),P('The completed work includes subject-disjoint dataset preparation, multiple pretrained backbone options, metric-learning training, cached embedding generation, persistent vector uploads and scripted retrieval experiments. A Streamlit demonstration operates on saved query metadata and invokes the query module; arbitrary image upload and an operational police database are proposed extensions. The forensic context motivates the project but the prototype has not been validated for criminal casework.'),H('1.5 Organisation of the report'),P('Following the reference thesis, Chapter 2 reviews related work, Chapter 3 establishes the background, Chapter 4 describes the proposed and implemented model, Chapter 5 documents experiments and testing, Chapter 6 presents measured results, and Chapter 7 gives conclusions and future scope.')]
story += chapter(2,'Literature Review')
story += [P('Fingerprint identification research spans friction-ridge evidence, feature extraction, biometric evaluation, and efficient similarity search. This review uses the cited sources to motivate design choices rather than claiming that results from one benchmark transfer to another.'),H('2.1 Friction-ridge comparison'),P('The National Institute of Justice Fingerprint Sourcebook describes ridge characteristics, development and preservation of impressions, documentation and examination methodology [1]. NIST highlights human factors in latent-print comparison [2]. These sources emphasise the importance of image quality, examination context and documented verification; they do not justify identifying an individual solely from a nearest-neighbour score.'),H('2.2 Learned fingerprint representations'),P('Engelsma, Cao and Jain propose DeepPrint, a compact fixed-length fingerprint representation incorporating domain knowledge such as alignment and minutiae detection [3]. Their system differs from the present project: this prototype uses a generic DenseNet backbone and does not implement DeepPrint alignment, minutiae supervision or the published large-scale protocol. The shared idea is to learn descriptors that support efficient vector comparison.'),H('2.3 CNN backbones and angular-margin learning'),P('DenseNet connects each layer to preceding layers within a dense block, encouraging feature reuse [4]. ResNet uses residual connections [5], whereas VGG explores deeper networks built from small convolution filters [6]. The repository implements all three as interchangeable backbones. ArcFace introduces an additive angular margin in a classification objective originally developed for face recognition [7]. The project adapts that objective to finger classes; the learned embedding head, rather than the training classifier, is used at inference.'),H('2.4 Dataset and research gap'),P('The SOCOFing paper describes 6,000 original fingerprints from 600 subjects together with labelled synthetic alterations [8]. Such alterations support controlled evaluation but are not equivalent to independently acquired latent crime-scene impressions. The key gap for this project is therefore external validity: low sample EER on held-out SOCOFing subjects must be followed by independent capture, operational-quality and open-set studies before any practical forensic use.'),H('2.5 Existing approaches and limitations')]
story += table('Table 2.1: Comparison of relevant approaches',[['Approach','Useful property','Limitation or distinction'],['Ridge and minutiae examination','Interpretable local evidence','Quality and examiner-dependent assessment [1], [2]'],['DeepPrint','Compact learned descriptor','Different architecture and evaluation protocol [3]'],['DenseNet + ArcFace (this project)','512-dimensional descriptor and vector search','Dataset-specific validation; no explicit minutiae model']],[130,150,150])
story += [P('The comparison illustrates a design trade-off: local ridge evidence can be examined and documented directly, whereas a learned descriptor provides a compact representation for retrieval. The present implementation prioritises a fixed-size representation and does not expose the individual ridge correspondences that a forensic examiner would ordinarily inspect. Further work could combine retrieval with a separately validated minutiae-based comparison.'), P('The differences in training data, capture conditions, galleries and evaluation protocols also prevent treating published results as direct baselines. This report therefore compares the project checkpoints only on identical sampled pairs and records the full protocol alongside the results.') ]
story += chapter(3,'Preliminaries')
story += [H('3.1 Fingerprint biometrics'),P('A finger impression records a portion of the friction-ridge surface. Global patterns such as arches, loops and whorls provide broad structure; local ridge endings, bifurcations and their spatial relationships provide finer detail. Acquisition can produce rolled, plain or latent impressions, with differences in pressure, rotation, coverage and background. This project operates on labelled dataset images rather than live sensor capture; the pipeline should not be assumed to handle operational latent impressions without validation [1], [2].'),H('3.2 Image preprocessing and quality'),P('The implemented preprocessing reads grayscale images, trims configured borders, optionally applies median denoising, applies CLAHE, optionally derives a crop using inverted Otsu thresholding and morphological closing, pads to a square and resizes to 128 by 128. Values are scaled to [0,1], cached as NumPy arrays and later replicated to three channels and normalised using ImageNet channel statistics. Cropping and denoising are configurable; their use must be established from the actual configuration rather than assumed.'),H('3.3 Convolutional neural networks'),P('A convolutional layer applies learned spatial filters to its input and generates feature maps. Nonlinear activations, normalisation and pooling enable successive layers to encode texture and broader spatial structure. Transfer learning starts from weights learned on a larger image dataset, then fine-tunes for fingerprint discrimination. The repository implements a projection from the backbone feature vector to a fixed embedding dimension followed by L2 normalisation.'),H('3.4 Embedding space and similarity'),P('Let f(x) be the CNN representation of preprocessed image x and W the learned projection. The system computes z = Wf(x) + b and e = z / ||z||2. A cosine score s(e1,e2) = e1 dot e2 lies in [-1,1] for nonzero unit vectors. A larger score indicates greater geometric similarity under the learned model; it is not a probability of identity or an evidential likelihood ratio.'),H('3.5 Verification and retrieval metrics'),P('Verification accepts a pair when its score crosses a threshold. FAR is the proportion of impostor pairs accepted; FRR is the proportion of genuine pairs rejected. EER is the point at which the two rates are equal or interpolated to equality. ROC AUC summarises discrimination over thresholds. For retrieval, precision at k is relevant items among the first k divided by k; recall at k is retrieved relevant items divided by all relevant gallery items. F1 is the harmonic mean of precision and recall. Average precision aggregates precision at relevant ranks and mAP averages that quantity across queries. The reported rank-position accuracy is different from recall at k or mAP.')]
story += chapter(4,'Proposed Model')
story += [P('The architecture combines an offline enrolment workflow with query-time retrieval. The experimental evaluation uses stored test or validation vectors as queries. The interface path for a new arbitrary uploaded image is a proposed integration, not a demonstrated end-to-end feature.'),H('4.1 Overall architecture')]
story += diagram('4.1','System architecture: implemented components and candidate review',['Fingerprint|image','Preprocess|128 x 128','DenseNet121|embedding','Pinecone|cosine search','Ranked|candidates'],'architecture.mmd')
story += [P('The model checkpoint and preprocessing configuration determine the vector space. Enrolment and querying must use the same version. Pinecone namespaces separate model and dataset splits; local manifests map each namespace to its checkpoint and metadata. A reviewer interprets candidate results outside the automated ranking workflow.'),H('4.2 Data-flow and workflow')]
story += diagram('4.2','Level 0 data flow: image to candidate list',['Dataset or|query source','Embedding|service','Pinecone|vector store','Search|results','Reviewer|assessment'],'dfd_level0.mmd')
story += [P('The external dataset supplies images and labels; the embedding process supplies vectors and limited metadata to the persistent vector store. A search request carries a vector to the same model-specific namespace and returns identifiers and similarity scores. Expert assessment is a contextual downstream activity, not an automated decision component.'),H('4.3 Preprocessing pipeline')]
story += diagram('4.3','Fingerprint preprocessing pipeline',['Grayscale|border trim','Optional|denoise','CLAHE|enhancement','Optional|ROI crop','Pad, resize|normalise'],'preprocessing.mmd')
story += [P('The optional ROI operation applies only when configured. Padding before resizing helps preserve aspect ratio. Image quality, missing ridges and severe geometric distortion can still affect the descriptor. Separate training augmentation produces variations during training; it is disabled when exporting evaluation embeddings.'),H('4.4 Dense feature extraction')]
story += diagram('4.4','DenseNet121 feature extraction and projection',['128 x 128|three channels','DenseNet121|dense blocks','Global average|pooling','Linear|512 values','L2|normalisation'],'dense_model.mmd')
story += [P('The selected checkpoint is checkpoints/densenet121_arcface/best.pt. DenseNet121 produces a 1,024-dimensional pooled feature; a learned linear head maps it to 512 dimensions and L2 normalisation yields the stored descriptor. ArcFace class weights are used during training, while inference returns the embedding. ResNet18 and VGG16 were retained as comparison backbones.'),H('4.5 Vector database design'),P('A Pinecone index is configured for 512-dimensional cosine vectors. Per-model and per-split namespaces reduce accidental mixing of incompatible embeddings. The uploader uses the split row as a stable vector ID and records class_id, split, model and available source-path and alteration metadata. The corresponding local metadata CSV records subject, hand and finger. These records are a logical mapping, not an implementation of a relational criminal-record database.')]
story += table('Table 4.1: Logical vector record',[['Field','Purpose'],['namespace','Model / split isolation'],['vector ID','Embedding row within the split'],['values','512 floating-point embedding components'],['class_id','Finger identity used in evaluation'],['split, model, source path','Provenance and reproducibility']],[130,300])
story += [H('4.6 Retrieval, sequence and user interaction')]
story += diagram('4.5','Search sequence for a saved query',['Select|query row','Load model|and vector','Search same|namespace','Map IDs|to class','Display|ranked list'],'sequence.mmd')
story += [P('The implemented command-line query loads a saved query row and the model, computes its embedding, asks Pinecone for top-k candidates and maps result identifiers to local gallery metadata. The separate person-evaluation script uses cached query embeddings and searches the image-level test or validation archive. The Streamlit demo selects an existing query number and top-k; an optional local Ollama explanation describes deterministic results and is not responsible for the match decision.'),H('4.7 Feasibility, requirements and deployment status'),P('Technical feasibility follows from the available Python, PyTorch, torchvision, OpenCV, NumPy, pandas, scikit-learn, Pinecone SDK and Streamlit components. A CUDA-capable NVIDIA GPU is useful for generating many embeddings but CPU inference is also supported. Pinecone requires account access, network connectivity, a compatible cosine index and a private API key. Storage and query costs depend on the service plan. Secure production enrolment, auditability, access controls and legal approval are required future engineering, not features demonstrated by the research scripts.'),H('4.8 Use cases and database relationships')]
story += diagram('4.6','Logical dataset and vector relationships',['Subject','Finger|class_id','Fingerprint|image','Model-specific|embedding','Pinecone|namespace'],'entity_relations.mmd')
story += [P('The implemented roles are a researcher who prepares data and checkpoints, an operator who runs exports or experiments, and a viewer who selects a stored demo query. A future authorised examiner could submit an acquired print and review candidates. An image belongs to one finger class and one split; each image has one vector per checkpoint. A person has multiple finger classes. These logical relationships support data integrity but no separate personnel identity registry has been implemented.')]
