story += [P('Figure 5.1 shows the partition into three subject-disjoint splits. Real images and synthetic alterations can occur in a split. Files and counts reflect the local metadata at the time of the experiments; they should be regenerated if the dataset changes.'),H('5.2 Training and inference'),P('The training implementation supports progressive unfreezing: an initial phase trains the embedding head while the backbone is frozen, followed by fine-tuning at a lower backbone learning rate. ArcFace and triplet objectives are available; the selected checkpoint uses ArcFace. The project compares best saved checkpoints rather than training new models for this report. Training hyperparameters and epoch counts vary across runs; no single unverified value is asserted here.'),P('Inference sets the model to evaluation mode, disables augmentation, computes embeddings using the trained head and validates normalisation. Local exports use memory-mapped arrays and per-split manifests. The uploader uses stable IDs, batched upserts, progress tracking, remote vector counts and sample fetch checks. The observed execution environment used an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 3080 Laptop GPU with 8 GB memory; timing was not measured as a formal benchmark.'),H('5.3 Verification experiment'),P('The model-comparison script sampled 1,000 distinct genuine pairs and 1,000 distinct impostor pairs from the processed test CSV with random seed 42. Genuine pairs are different images sharing class_id; impostor pairs have different class_id. All twelve best checkpoints scored the same 2,000 pairs using cosine similarity. There were 3,179 unique participating images. EER is obtained by linear interpolation of the empirical ROC crossing; the separately reported nearest operating threshold is derived from the same test scores and should not be reused as a deployment threshold.'),H('5.4 Retrieval experiment'),P('For the DenseNet evaluation, 100 people were sampled reproducibly: 52 from test and 48 from validation. One random image was selected for each of ten fingers, producing 1,000 queries. Each query searched only its own complete split namespace with top_k=11. The exact query vector ID was removed. The first, third and fifth remaining candidates correspond to original positions 2, 4 and 6 when the self-match is ranked first. Every query in this run returned the self-vector first. The candidate class_id was compared to the query finger class_id; each person has ten trials per position.'),P('This experiment searches the same collection from which each query was drawn. Removing self avoids the trivial identical-vector result, but other altered versions of the same source finger remain in the gallery. It measures closed-set finger retrieval under this sampled protocol; it is neither an independent-impression study nor an open-set false-identification assessment.'),H('5.5 Testing methodology and checks')]
